00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.210
Most apps are pretty read heavy.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:12.112
So I thought that might
mimic your users' behavior.

00:01:12.112 --> 00:01:13.320
MARTIN OMANDER: 5 to 1, yeah.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.060
That ratio sounds about right.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:17.160
CRAIG LABENZ: OK, great.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:21.910
In that case, you can run
locust-f locustfile.py

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:22.945
to start the process.

00:01:22.945 --> 00:01:24.070
MARTIN OMANDER: All righty.

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:25.948
Starting it.

00:01:25.948 --> 00:01:26.740
CRAIG LABENZ: Cool.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:28.310
And the Locust server
is now running.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.852
So if you click on the link it
just printed in your terminal,

00:01:30.852 --> 00:01:32.315
we'll be ready to
start the test.

00:01:32.315 --> 00:01:33.440
MARTIN OMANDER: All righty.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:34.445
Open that one.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:39.290
Ah, OK.

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:41.180
Number of users.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:43.010
I'll start with 50.

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:45.718
Spawn rate, what's that?

00:01:45.718 --> 00:01:47.260
CRAIG LABENZ: The
spawn rate controls

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:49.570
how quickly Locust should
reach full throttle.

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:51.460
So if you enter a
value of 10, Locust

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:53.857
would reach 50 concurrent
users in five seconds.

00:01:53.857 --> 00:01:54.940
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:55.600
Cool.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:59.860
And then for your
URL, I will grab that

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:05.110
from the live URL over
here and paste that in,

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:06.760
and this is neat.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.395
I'm going to release the swarm.

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:12.850
OK.

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:18.414
So it's sending data to the
app if I click here on charts.

00:02:18.414 --> 00:02:20.380
Ah, right.

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:24.220
Here I see the
traffic ramping up.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:27.790
Response times, OK.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:31.690
Bit of a long response time
right there in the beginning

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:34.230
but then it's plateauing out.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:35.840
Pretty good response times.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.990
The subsecond response times.

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:40.360
It looks like it's holding
up pretty well, especially

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.820
since we deployed
this app to Europe.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:45.970
And we're running this
test from California.

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:48.430
But we should take
a peek at how things

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:50.780
are running on the server.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:52.240
Let's see here.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:53.575
We'll go into Cloud Run.

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:56.690
All right.

00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:00.590
So I'm scrolling down here,
checking out the container

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:02.960
memory utilization.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:05.780
Looks like it's at a
pretty healthy level.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:07.700
But of course, Cloud
Run is serverless.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:11.060
So it would add more
resources if they were needed.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:14.937
So Cloud Run should
really always be healthy.

00:03:14.937 --> 00:03:15.770
CRAIG LABENZ: Right.

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:18.170
And to that point, the real
question we're here to answer

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:20.440
is how your database
is holding up.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:21.620
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah, OK.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:24.080
Switching to the database
in the console now.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.100
Going into the poll database.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:33.940
CPU was very low.

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:35.990
Then we created the database.

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:37.860
So that took a lot of CPU.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.090
Then it was very low
here when it did nothing.

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:42.670
And then we started the
test right around here.

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:45.540
It looks like CPU is still
pretty low even though we're

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:48.018
putting a lot of load on it.

00:03:48.018 --> 00:03:49.185
Let's have a look at memory.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:55.990
Memory usage is holding
steady around 600 megabytes.

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:59.140
And our server has
3.75 gigabytes.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:00.923
So memory is also healthy.

00:04:00.923 --> 00:04:03.340
CRAIG LABENZ: And remember,
since you're not launched yet,

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:05.170
if it shows high
CPU or memory, it

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:07.960
would be very easy to increase
those for your database

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:11.050
while there are still no
users to care about downtime.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:12.890
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, sounds good.

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:14.920
But what if I have
launched my app

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:16.420
and the database is struggling?

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:18.880
What should I do then?

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.760
CRAIG LABENZ: I usually follow
a four step plan to ensure

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:23.920
that my apps perform well.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.192
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh,
I love ordered lists.

00:04:26.192 --> 00:04:28.150
CRAIG LABENZ: First, the
obvious place to start

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:30.880
is optimizing your
actual database queries.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:33.370
Things like making sure every
column involved in a where

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:35.620
clause has an underlying
database index,

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:39.010
or that frequently run
expensive queries like counts,

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:41.465
are denormalized if possible.

00:04:41.465 --> 00:04:43.340
MARTIN OMANDER: That's
great to keep in mind.

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:45.370
But what should I do
if my app is still

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:49.357
slow after optimizing
all of my queries?

00:04:49.357 --> 00:04:51.440
CRAIG LABENZ: It's true
that optimization can only

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:52.610
get you so far.

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:55.820
So the second step is to
simply add more CPUs or memory

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:57.110
to your database instance.

00:04:57.110 --> 00:05:00.100
This is often the simplest fix.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:02.000
However, even that
runs into limits.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:03.973
For example, making your
main database larger

00:05:03.973 --> 00:05:05.390
won't necessarily
help an app that

00:05:05.390 --> 00:05:08.660
performs well in one hemisphere
but poorly in the other.

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:11.030
And so my third step is
to add read replicas,

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:13.508
potentially in different regions
if your users are global.

00:05:13.508 --> 00:05:14.300
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:15.650
This makes sense so far.

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:17.270
And it sounds like
these steps would

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:19.010
accommodate most applications.

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:20.565
What's the last step?

00:05:20.565 --> 00:05:22.940
CRAIG LABENZ: The last step
is to introduce partitioning,

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:24.227
or sharding, to your database.

00:05:24.227 --> 00:05:26.060
But you should never
attempt these solutions

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:28.620
until you've exhausted all
of your simpler options.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:31.190
Because these are the most
complicated to do correctly.

00:05:31.190 --> 00:05:33.320
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, that
list is really helpful.

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:35.990
My users incessantly
refresh the page

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:38.910
to track which team is
winning tabs or spaces.

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:41.660
So I'm optimistic
about read replicas.

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:42.802
Could we set one up?

00:05:42.802 --> 00:05:43.760
CRAIG LABENZ: For sure.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:47.090
To set up a read replica, click
on your Cloud SQL instance

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:49.067
and then click on the
replicas tab on the left.

00:05:49.067 --> 00:05:50.150
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:52.620
Replicas.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:53.820
Yep.

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:55.710
CRAIG LABENZ: Select
the Create Read Replica

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:56.812
button in the middle.

00:05:56.812 --> 00:05:57.895
MARTIN OMANDER: Very nice.

00:06:01.708 --> 00:06:04.250
CRAIG LABENZ: And all of these
default values should be good.

00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:05.260
So you can press Create.

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:06.343
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:11.270
CRAIG LABENZ: Now this will
take several minutes to catch up

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:12.890
to your production database.

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:16.010
Now that your replica is ready,
navigate back to your Cloud Run

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:18.140
service and click on
the Edit and Deploy

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:19.190
a New Revision button.

00:06:23.487 --> 00:06:24.570
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:27.240
Here's my poll service.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:29.430
I'm clicking it.

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.090
Edit and deploy new version.

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:31.590
All right.

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:32.580
And I'm clicking it.

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:37.875
CRAIG LABENZ: And on that page,
select the Connections tab.

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:43.330
MARTIN OMANDER: Connections.

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:44.890
Very good.

00:06:44.890 --> 00:06:46.858
Cloud SQL connections?

00:06:46.858 --> 00:06:47.650
CRAIG LABENZ: Yeah.

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:50.650
Right there, you can
press Add Connection

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:53.410
and select your new replica.

00:06:53.410 --> 00:06:57.045
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, there's
my poll database replica.

00:06:57.045 --> 00:06:58.670
CRAIG LABENZ: And
once you deploy this,

00:06:58.670 --> 00:07:01.850
you'll be ready to route
your select queries

00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:03.890
to that replica in
your application code

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:05.660
as you see fit.

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:07.580
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh,
that sounds excellent.

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:09.800
Do you have any advice
on how to do that,

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:11.900
where to route queries?

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:12.650
CRAIG LABENZ: Yes.

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:14.420
First and foremost,
read replicas

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:18.080
can only receive read
queries, so select statements.

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:21.770
Insert or update queries cannot
go to your read replicas.

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:22.670
They will fail.

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:25.530
And they must go to
the primary database.

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:28.370
Now just be aware if you
write to the main database

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:30.080
and then immediately
trying to read

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:32.270
those same rows
from your replica,

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:34.260
you may not get the latest data.

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:38.210
And in this situation, you must
also send those select queries

00:07:38.210 --> 00:07:40.307
to your main database.

00:07:40.307 --> 00:07:41.390
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:44.640
I'll work the read replica
into my application code later.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:47.370
For now, I think
I'm ready to launch.

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:48.620
CRAIG LABENZ: Congratulations.

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:50.690
Your MVP is out the
door and now begins

00:07:50.690 --> 00:07:55.457
the long process of adding
new bugs, I mean features.

00:07:55.457 --> 00:07:57.290
MARTIN OMANDER: Maybe
you write bugs, Craig.

00:07:57.290 --> 00:07:59.827
But I prefer not to.

00:07:59.827 --> 00:08:01.910
CRAIG LABENZ: Well, if you
prefer not adding bugs,

00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:04.700
then you might want to plan
ahead for your eventual changes

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:06.080
to your database schema.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:08.540
With a distributed
infrastructure like Cloud Run,

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:11.330
it's critical to keep
database schema updates out

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:13.520
of your application
startup code.

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:14.762
MARTIN OMANDER: Why is that?

00:08:14.762 --> 00:08:16.220
CRAIG LABENZ: Well,
some developers

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:19.250
run database schema updates like
creating new tables or columns

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:21.320
as part of their
application launch.

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:23.360
But for example, let's
say the next version

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:26.730
of your application
needs a country table.

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:29.690
If this table is created by
your framework when it starts,

00:08:29.690 --> 00:08:32.720
multiple Cloud Run instances
could simultaneously

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:34.850
detect that the table
is missing and attempt

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:36.470
to create and populate it.

00:08:36.470 --> 00:08:38.120
Some of those queries
could fail or you

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:39.578
could end up with
a countries table

00:08:39.578 --> 00:08:41.150
full of duplicate records.

00:08:41.150 --> 00:08:42.530
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, I see.

00:08:42.530 --> 00:08:44.600
I usually run all of
my database schema

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:47.000
changes a separate
Cloud build step

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:49.233
when I build my new container.

00:08:49.233 --> 00:08:50.150
CRAIG LABENZ: Perfect.

00:08:50.150 --> 00:08:52.675
That's definitely the
right way to handle it.

00:08:52.675 --> 00:08:53.550
MARTIN OMANDER: Cool.

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:56.570
I feel a lot more confident
about pairing Cloud SQL

00:08:56.570 --> 00:08:58.370
and Cloud Run now.

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:01.310
Let's see if I can remember
all of the important tips.

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:05.690
First, I load test and profile
my app to measure performance.

00:09:05.690 --> 00:09:08.900
As needed, I apply the
optimization steps.

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:11.900
Second, to avoid
conflict I make sure

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:14.030
that my database
scheme updates don't

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:15.910
run on application startup.

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:16.910
CRAIG LABENZ: That's it.

00:09:16.910 --> 00:09:19.670
With those steps,
Cloud Run and Cloud SQL

00:09:19.670 --> 00:09:23.690
can be a great pairing like
cheese and wine or peas

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:24.950
and carrots.

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:28.605
Or dare I say, like
tabs and spaces.

00:09:28.605 --> 00:09:29.480
MARTIN OMANDER: What?

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:30.200
No.

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:31.882
You don't mix tabs and spaces.

00:09:31.882 --> 00:09:33.090
What's wrong with you, Craig?

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:35.240
[MUSIC PLAYING]

